{"custom_id": "9941bc184c76780601982b4b9e5e3a6789f4746a72861932", "meta": {"loop": "topk1L07", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "5e654e85961633ce37889e9cd453748ab7d3340a0abe06efdaac40323c934690", "prompt_chars": 10945}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_01GeJV3AQhMwE2XppFHTn5c8", "saved_utc": "2026-10-23T11:33:05+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgK5z4VotbFZAZyBLPZhj", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm looking at the gold vs LLM label discrepancies involving disease names embedded as modifiers within gene/protein names, like \"breast cancer\" inside \"BRCA1\" and \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome\" inside its protein name—cases where the disease term functions as a compound noun modifier rather than a standalone entity.\n\nComparing this to true positives like \"ovarian cancer\" modifying \"population\" or \"risk,\" I'm realizing the distinguishing factor is that the head noun in the discrepant cases is itself a gene or protein name, meaning the disease string is embedded inside a biomolecule entity name rather than modifying a generic noun.\n\nOther confirming cases: \"SLS\", \"G6PD deficient phenotype\" are labeled Modifier because their heads are clinical/phenotype nouns rather than gene names, reinforcing that disease spans get tagged Modifier when qualifying population/clinical nouns but get suppressed to O when embedded inside a gene/protein name string, as seen with \"breast cancer susceptibility gene 1.\"\n\nSo the real trigger seems to be nomenclature structure: when a disease name premodifies a head like \"gene\" or \"protein,\" especially followed by an acronym in parentheses (e.g., \"BRCA1,\" \"WASP\"), the whole phrase reads as a molecular entity name and the model suppresses the disease substring to O. TPs never have this gene/protein head, confirming the pattern, though gold annotation still marks the embedded disease string as Modifier rather than absorbing it into the gene span — so the fix is to keep tagging disease substrings as Modifier even inside gene/protein nomenclature, leaving only the gene-specific tokens (\"gene,\" \"protein,\" \"1\") as O. I'm now drafting the final Pattern Insight entry with this Confusion Trigger, Contrastive Evidence, and Linguistic Rule, keeping the explanation brief before the single-item Markdown list.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** In the discrepancies, the disease name is a premodifier inside a gene or protein name: \"breast cancer susceptibility gene 1 (BRCA1)\" and \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASP)\". In the True Positives, the disease name premodifies a clinical or epidemiological head: \"ovarian cancer cases / population / study / risk\", \"G6PD deficient phenotype\", \"fragile X syndrome patients\". The separating feature is whether the head noun is a molecular entity (gene, protein) or a clinical entity (patients, cases, population, phenotype).\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The head nouns \"gene\" and \"protein\", often followed by an acronym in parentheses (BRCA1, WASP), frame the whole span as a gene or protein identifier. The model appears to treat the full string as one molecular name and assigns O to the embedded disease tokens.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** All three discrepancies share a gene/protein head noun with a nomenclature-style expansion. Example 2 and Example 3 are the same entity in the same document, so the cluster covers two distinct sentence contexts. No True Positive has a gene or protein head, so the trigger separates the two sets. The hypothesis explains all discrepancies without needing any additional rule.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Insight:** Disease names embedded in gene/protein nomenclature are missed.\n  - **Pattern Name:** Disease-Name Premodifier Inside Gene/Protein Nomenclature\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A disease name is a premodifier of a gene or protein head noun (e.g., \"gene\", \"protein\", often followed by an acronym in parentheses). The model treats the entire compound as a single non-disease gene/protein identifier and suppresses the embedded disease span to O.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positives such as \"ovarian cancer cases\", \"ovarian cancer population\", \"G6PD deficient phenotype\", and \"fragile X syndrome patients\" also have a disease premodifier, but the head is clinical or population-level, not molecular. The model correctly labels these as Modifier, so the disease term is not absorbed into a non-disease span.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Disease-name tokens remain disease mentions even when they are embedded in a gene or protein compound. When a disease term premodifies a gene, protein, or other molecular head, label the disease substring as Modifier and leave the molecular head tokens (e.g., \"gene\", \"1\", \"protein\") as O. Do not let the molecular head suppress the disease label.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4310, "output_tokens": 4473, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 3571}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}